1
00:00:14,823 --> 00:00:18,026
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this is the beginning of the Satipaṭṭhāna Sutta.

2
00:00:18,326 --> 00:00:20,668
[SPEAKER_02]: This is Majjhima Nikāya 10.

3
00:00:21,989 --> 00:00:22,689
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I have heard.

4
00:00:23,470 --> 00:00:29,415
[SPEAKER_02]: At one time, the Buddha was staying in the land of the Kurus, near the Kuru town, named Khamasadamma.

5
00:00:30,142 --> 00:00:31,743
[SPEAKER_01]: There, the Buddha addressed the bhikkhus.

6
00:00:31,983 --> 00:00:34,125
[SPEAKER_02]: Bhikkhus, Bhante, they replied.

7
00:00:34,605 --> 00:00:35,466
[SPEAKER_02]: The Buddha said this.

8
00:00:36,266 --> 00:00:53,638
[SPEAKER_02]: Bhikkhus, this is the path that leads squarely to the purification of beings, to getting past sorrow and lamentation, to make an end of suffering and upset, to accomplishing the method, and to realizing nibbāna, namely, the four establishments of recollection.

9
00:00:54,499 --> 00:01:06,266
[SPEAKER_02]: Here, Abhiku abides maintaining perspective of the body concurrently with the body, diligent, aware, and recollected, having dispelled longing and upset in regard to the world.

10
00:01:06,966 --> 00:01:16,072
[SPEAKER_02]: He abides maintaining perspective of feelings concurrently with feelings, diligent, aware, and recollected, having dispelled longing and upset in regard to the world.

11
00:01:16,866 --> 00:01:20,487
[SPEAKER_02]: He abides maintaining perspective of the mind concurrently with the mind.

12
00:01:20,987 --> 00:01:22,868
[SPEAKER_02]: Diligent, aware, and recollected.

13
00:01:23,368 --> 00:01:26,408
[SPEAKER_02]: Having dispelled longing and upset in regard to the world.

14
00:01:26,949 --> 00:01:30,650
[SPEAKER_02]: He abides maintaining perspective of phenomena concurrently with phenomena.

15
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:32,830
[SPEAKER_02]: Diligent, aware, and recollected.

16
00:01:33,210 --> 00:01:36,011
[SPEAKER_02]: Having dispelled longing and upset in regard to the world.

17
00:01:46,252 --> 00:01:49,596
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you practice that?

18
00:01:50,478 --> 00:01:51,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Concretely?

19
00:01:55,604 --> 00:01:56,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Any ideas?

20
00:02:20,586 --> 00:02:30,656
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you would have to start by understanding the concurrency that is a common thread with all the four establishments of recollection.

21
00:02:32,458 --> 00:02:40,767
[SPEAKER_03]: And perhaps we can clear up from the outset that virtue and sensory strength are already prerequisites.

22
00:02:41,388 --> 00:02:42,649
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't hurt to mention that again.

23
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:54,038
[SPEAKER_03]: So, assuming that that is understood, then one needs to understand that concurrent attention as a principle that all these satipaṭṭhāna have in common.

24
00:02:56,580 --> 00:03:10,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because I think I mentioned in one of the previous ones that it's easy for people to think of satipaṭṭhāna as absorption or focusing because they overemphasize the body.

25
00:03:11,690 --> 00:03:26,441
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's talking there about these four aspects and feelings, like it says, if you read further, it says, because feelings, then, in the same, along the same lines, feelings are interpreted as sensations.

26
00:03:27,322 --> 00:03:32,266
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you look further in the sutta, it says you have feelings of the flesh, feelings not of the flesh.

27
00:03:33,226 --> 00:03:34,928
[SPEAKER_01]: So that, right from the outset,

28
00:03:36,509 --> 00:03:50,615
[SPEAKER_01]: This qualifies the interpretation of feelings as sensations, which means, like, if you have something that's not of the flesh, something that's not, like, in your knees, or in your back, or in your belly, by definition, that's not something you'll be able to focus on.

29
00:03:51,255 --> 00:03:56,657
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as it says there, it's, well, as I translate it, it's a maintaining perspective.

30
00:03:58,218 --> 00:04:03,900
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we could maybe ask, why does it say there that

31
00:04:05,703 --> 00:04:16,851
[SPEAKER_01]: These four foundations of recollection would help you to transcend suffering, transcend sorrow, lamentation, and, you know, overcoming upset and longing in regard to the world.

32
00:04:18,272 --> 00:04:22,035
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not focused, of course, you will overcome.

33
00:04:23,627 --> 00:04:29,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Longing in all states by just replacing what you perceive with something else.

34
00:04:30,372 --> 00:04:36,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you kind of fix the mess that you have in the room by not being there to begin with.

35
00:04:36,375 --> 00:04:39,878
[SPEAKER_01]: You just go to another room and then you don't have to see what's there.

36
00:04:40,358 --> 00:04:43,120
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but which means that fundamentally you don't really fix it.

37
00:04:43,180 --> 00:04:43,580
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

38
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:45,081
[SPEAKER_03]: You're just sticking your head in the sand.

39
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:50,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but so if it's not that, then how does this actually

40
00:04:51,465 --> 00:05:01,736
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so how does maintaining perspective in these four distinct ways end soul lamentation, pain, grief and despair?

41
00:05:02,377 --> 00:05:03,238
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the question, right?

42
00:05:04,299 --> 00:05:04,439
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

43
00:05:04,459 --> 00:05:10,425
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's that kind of, I mean, I guess, a little insert before we really delve into that question.

44
00:05:12,222 --> 00:05:32,378
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it talks about these four establishments, and it says, having put aside longing and despair, so that it kind of... the way I read that is, it kind of says that having... like the putting that aside is almost your gateway into this practice.

45
00:05:32,838 --> 00:05:40,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because always in the suttas, there's many suttas that say that practice of mindfulness is preceded by sense restraint.

46
00:05:41,388 --> 00:05:47,990
[SPEAKER_01]: And sense-restraint, as defined, is exactly the same term in Pali that talks about longing and upset.

47
00:05:48,130 --> 00:05:55,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, not grasping signs and features means that you won't be overcome with longing and upset regarding the objects of the six senses.

48
00:05:55,752 --> 00:05:57,873
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like the prerequisite for satipaṭṭhāna.

49
00:05:58,453 --> 00:06:00,013
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the result of satipaṭṭhāna.

50
00:06:00,133 --> 00:06:05,915
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, you need to already not be doing this as a form of management.

51
00:06:06,829 --> 00:06:12,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have dealt with the coarse longing and upset beforehand, through sense restraint.

52
00:06:13,231 --> 00:06:14,812
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not doing...

53
00:06:14,832 --> 00:06:22,654
[SPEAKER_01]: If you skip that aspect, then, automatically, this will just become a way of, like, how do I replace this or get rid of it as quickly as possible.

54
00:06:22,834 --> 00:06:24,835
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you're not really maintaining perspective.

55
00:06:24,875 --> 00:06:25,595
[SPEAKER_01]: You're kind of just...

56
00:06:26,269 --> 00:06:31,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Replacing one thing with another, but the perspective remains equally muddled.

57
00:06:31,894 --> 00:06:47,306
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's a lot of people might go into this practice looking for the way out of longing and upset, but actually overcoming longing and upset to some degree is kind of your... that's the work that needs to be done before you even really think about satipaṭṭhāna properly.

58
00:06:51,112 --> 00:07:02,099
[SPEAKER_01]: That's, I think, Samyutta Nikaya 46.6, we can put it in the description, that talks about how, yes, mindfulness is preceded by sensor strength.

59
00:07:02,159 --> 00:07:06,582
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're already established in sensor strength, then you can practice the force of nipaṭṭhānas.

60
00:07:07,162 --> 00:07:19,070
[SPEAKER_01]: But so, having said that then, how does that cement and fully establish your mind in freedom from any kind of suffering?

61
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:23,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you could ask it the other way.

62
00:07:23,661 --> 00:07:27,363
[SPEAKER_01]: How does not having mindfulness make you suffer?

63
00:07:49,776 --> 00:07:51,477
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's longing and upset?

64
00:07:55,053 --> 00:07:55,954
[SPEAKER_01]: What is sorrow?

65
00:07:56,214 --> 00:07:58,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it just objects that come to you?

66
00:08:06,823 --> 00:08:10,206
[SPEAKER_04]: It's more like my attitude toward this object.

67
00:08:14,149 --> 00:08:20,474
[SPEAKER_03]: And more specifically, like, based on what attitude would there be sorrow and despair and so on?

68
00:08:21,001 --> 00:08:21,561
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm craving.

69
00:08:21,821 --> 00:08:23,902
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm craving for things to be not the way it is.

70
00:08:24,843 --> 00:08:26,444
[SPEAKER_04]: Just wanting things to be different.

71
00:08:28,485 --> 00:08:30,786
[SPEAKER_02]: And it doesn't say destroying, longing and upset.

72
00:08:31,586 --> 00:08:43,992
[SPEAKER_02]: It's more... kind of those... not continually acting out of these tendencies.

73
00:08:44,872 --> 00:08:47,954
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's like... that's not to say there won't still be

74
00:08:49,385 --> 00:08:51,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Inclinations towards old habits.

75
00:08:52,149 --> 00:09:02,144
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's... it's not just like, oh, you know, I still sometimes, like, have the thought that I should, whatever, smoke a cigarette.

76
00:09:02,184 --> 00:09:04,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, gosh, that means I haven't kind of...

77
00:09:05,322 --> 00:09:06,583
[SPEAKER_02]: I haven't gotten it yet, you know.

78
00:09:06,763 --> 00:09:09,726
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe a few more years, that'll eventually kind of wear out.

79
00:09:09,806 --> 00:09:13,849
[SPEAKER_02]: I've noticed over the past few years, you know, you get into that kind of stuff.

80
00:09:13,929 --> 00:09:17,833
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's like the the setting it down is something you should be able to do immediately.

81
00:09:18,633 --> 00:09:23,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and and you look at the description of mindfulness of the mind, and it doesn't say

82
00:09:24,883 --> 00:09:26,764
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't have passion.

83
00:09:27,485 --> 00:09:28,505
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't have aversion.

84
00:09:29,286 --> 00:09:36,030
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, when it's there, he knows it as such, and in itself, that's an establishment of perspective.

85
00:09:36,870 --> 00:09:38,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Because...

86
00:09:38,191 --> 00:09:43,354
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what I was trying to get at is... Yeah, so, it's the craving that makes these objects a problem.

87
00:09:44,295 --> 00:09:45,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means that if you...

88
00:09:46,636 --> 00:09:50,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, if you replace the objects, the craving won't be there.

89
00:09:50,538 --> 00:09:51,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like...

90
00:09:52,572 --> 00:09:56,855
[SPEAKER_01]: The push for the craving is not there anymore, but that doesn't mean you're not liable to the craving.

91
00:09:57,575 --> 00:10:06,361
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, if you, instead of going at that route, you realize it's through lack of perspective that craving comes to be.

92
00:10:07,622 --> 00:10:12,906
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not through just in itself, kind of, this comes to me or something.

93
00:10:13,026 --> 00:10:15,968
[SPEAKER_01]: It's immediately, when you don't have perspective,

94
00:10:16,955 --> 00:10:18,816
[SPEAKER_01]: You act out, on some level.

95
00:10:19,256 --> 00:10:28,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're establishing sense restraint and so on, longing and upset, you're not acting out of it anymore.

96
00:10:29,322 --> 00:10:37,446
[SPEAKER_01]: But the perversion of perception, you could say, or the perversion of the wrong order of seeing things, is still there.

97
00:10:37,686 --> 00:10:40,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the feeling that you might be having

98
00:10:41,243 --> 00:10:52,365
[SPEAKER_01]: For example, we could take that example as... so as to, sort of, eh... illustrate the principle in a way that people probably not familiar with, because it's usually the body that's... that's emphasized, as I just said.

99
00:10:52,985 --> 00:10:57,546
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's say you have a unpleasant feeling that's enduring right now.

100
00:10:57,666 --> 00:10:59,766
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're established in virtue, you're not acting out of it.

101
00:11:01,046 --> 00:11:09,468
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh... if you're not aware that there is an unpleasant feeling enduring for me right now, as the sutta says,

102
00:11:10,879 --> 00:11:12,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Automatically, you will be

103
00:11:14,017 --> 00:11:27,940
[SPEAKER_01]: On some level, inclining towards either getting rid of it, or indulging in it, whatever it wants you to do, you should have at least welcomed that direction, even if you're not doing it physically or verbally.

104
00:11:28,661 --> 00:11:33,382
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you see, actually, not just, how do I get rid of this?

105
00:11:33,482 --> 00:11:33,802
[SPEAKER_01]: But, no.

106
00:11:34,402 --> 00:11:41,724
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a feeling, unpleasant feeling, enduring in this experience, and I maintain perspective of that.

107
00:11:41,804 --> 00:11:42,644
[SPEAKER_01]: That perspective

108
00:11:44,194 --> 00:11:47,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Faith, in itself, prevents you from acting out of it.

109
00:11:48,504 --> 00:11:52,693
[SPEAKER_01]: But you need to, to some extent, that's why they said to say, faith needs to come first.

110
00:11:54,595 --> 00:11:58,357
[SPEAKER_01]: As a faculty, it even comes before mindfulness or before recollection.

111
00:11:58,957 --> 00:12:17,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, eh... if you haven't taken it on faith, that that is the middle way, that it is just this maintaining a perspective that will truly uproot the underlying tendencies you're craving, then your mind will convince you like, no, no, no, I have to deal with it, I have to do something about it.

112
00:12:17,807 --> 00:12:22,189
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, you will be going with your old habits, which is trying to deal with feelings.

113
00:12:22,269 --> 00:12:22,530
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

114
00:12:23,830 --> 00:12:36,813
[SPEAKER_04]: Can we say, like, just to see if I understood what you say, like, so the feeling is there, regardless we like it or not, but ignorance in regard to it, it's something that we actively engage with, that direction of ignoring that feeling.

115
00:12:37,013 --> 00:12:38,093
[SPEAKER_01]: You have a say in it, yeah.

116
00:12:38,273 --> 00:12:45,855
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, we have a say in it, but it's... if we don't see this feeling, it's because we, ourself, engage in that direction of ignorance.

117
00:12:46,095 --> 00:12:46,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

118
00:12:46,956 --> 00:12:52,957
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the more you act out, then the harder it becomes to recognize it, the more the ignorance increases.

119
00:12:53,998 --> 00:13:05,847
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why, then, it says in the Parittisana Upāda, the dependent origination formula or series of steps, it says, with ignorance, there is activity.

120
00:13:06,427 --> 00:13:07,308
[SPEAKER_01]: There is acting out.

121
00:13:07,448 --> 00:13:10,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, of the wrong kind, not like just anything in general.

122
00:13:10,831 --> 00:13:18,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... all it takes for these unwholesome things to be there is not seeing and not seeing in this proper sense of this.

123
00:13:19,438 --> 00:13:21,860
[SPEAKER_01]: You could say it's like lack of clarity.

124
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:30,882
[SPEAKER_01]: These general aspects of your experience make you descend onto the particulars of the experience.

125
00:13:31,482 --> 00:13:36,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Descending onto particulars is what unwholesomeness is, in and of itself.

126
00:13:36,703 --> 00:13:51,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's the right ideas about dhamma, or something like that, the fact that you are just absorbed in that, and you don't see the more general component of that experience then and there, means you will be acting out of craving.

127
00:13:51,947 --> 00:13:52,127
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

128
00:13:52,387 --> 00:14:01,793
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, since being with the particulars is the problem there, then body, as a satipaṭṭhāna, cannot be a particular.

129
00:14:02,154 --> 00:14:05,396
[SPEAKER_03]: Feeling, as a satipaṭṭhāna, cannot be a particular sensation.

130
00:14:05,996 --> 00:14:10,479
[SPEAKER_03]: Mind, as a particular, or mood, cannot be random thoughts that you happen to have.

131
00:14:10,959 --> 00:14:11,700
[SPEAKER_03]: And dhamma,

132
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:15,981
[SPEAKER_03]: As a general phenomenon cannot be anything particular.

133
00:14:16,402 --> 00:14:19,943
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they all come with, let's say, a relative totality.

134
00:14:20,623 --> 00:14:24,304
[SPEAKER_03]: And that needs to be discerned, which is the perspective.

135
00:14:25,025 --> 00:14:36,809
[SPEAKER_03]: And perhaps we can find out why, when that perspective is established, how, when there is that totality, when one establishes one's mind that way, why there is no room for craving then to interfere.

136
00:14:37,869 --> 00:14:39,049
[SPEAKER_03]: How is that?

137
00:14:39,169 --> 00:14:46,832
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is it that when that perspective is lost, and you dwell on the particulars, that it by definition means craving, which is the suffering?

138
00:14:47,432 --> 00:14:56,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the thing is, you wouldn't be able to explain, like, why, in a sort of external sense, as if you get out of your experience and then examine how it happens.

139
00:14:56,955 --> 00:14:57,155
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

140
00:14:57,435 --> 00:15:04,257
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, at any given time, you are either absorbed in the particulars, or you are established in the right perspective.

141
00:15:04,277 --> 00:15:04,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

142
00:15:04,657 --> 00:15:06,098
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you would get to see that then,

143
00:15:07,478 --> 00:15:17,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you will need to have faith in that the Buddha is saying, these things are overcome through perspective, not through directly tackling them head-on.

144
00:15:17,981 --> 00:15:21,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's when you get into... well, you fall from the middle way.

145
00:15:21,202 --> 00:15:26,503
[SPEAKER_01]: As the sutta says... as the first sutta of the Samyutta Nikaya says, when you push forward,

146
00:15:28,023 --> 00:15:29,765
[SPEAKER_01]: You get carried away by the flood.

147
00:15:30,145 --> 00:15:32,146
[SPEAKER_01]: When you stand still, you sink.

148
00:15:32,847 --> 00:15:35,269
[SPEAKER_01]: So, pushing forward means like I'm trying to get rid of this.

149
00:15:35,329 --> 00:15:36,930
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm trying to replace it with something else.

150
00:15:37,530 --> 00:15:40,592
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, standing still is like you just give up everything.

151
00:15:40,632 --> 00:15:41,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Just that I don't make any effort.

152
00:15:41,993 --> 00:15:46,557
[SPEAKER_01]: I just relax in choiceless awareness or something like that.

153
00:15:46,577 --> 00:15:47,978
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, it is an effort.

154
00:15:48,818 --> 00:15:53,779
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is no right mindfulness without right effort, because that's how the path works.

155
00:15:53,879 --> 00:15:58,941
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's not an effort in the sense of changing the content of what you're experiencing.

156
00:15:59,521 --> 00:16:03,162
[SPEAKER_01]: It's seeing that general nature that sits behind that content.

157
00:16:03,302 --> 00:16:14,684
[SPEAKER_01]: And as you said, the body has to be understood on that level as well, because the body is like... That's actually the purpose of breathing, for example, which is the very first

158
00:16:17,038 --> 00:16:26,424
[SPEAKER_01]: That item that comes up in the mindfulness of the body, it's... breathing is that general thing that's always there, like, regardless of the content of your experience.

159
00:16:27,262 --> 00:16:36,150
[SPEAKER_01]: The one thing that you know for sure, if you're still alive, is that breathing will be there, underneath everything else that's happening.

160
00:16:36,250 --> 00:16:40,514
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, that unabsorbs you from these particulars of your experience.

161
00:16:41,214 --> 00:16:47,019
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's actually, like, precisely the point not to focus on it.

162
00:16:47,139 --> 00:16:48,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Because something that's general,

163
00:16:49,664 --> 00:16:52,967
[SPEAKER_01]: necessarily is defined by its relation to particulars.

164
00:16:53,447 --> 00:16:56,269
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you focus on it, you turn it into a new particular.

165
00:16:56,470 --> 00:16:57,871
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's not the context anymore.

166
00:16:57,971 --> 00:17:00,353
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, something else will be the background for that.

167
00:17:01,093 --> 00:17:05,397
[SPEAKER_01]: And the point, as well, is the perspective.

168
00:17:05,957 --> 00:17:12,863
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not even something that you can do every second, if it's really a perspective.

169
00:17:13,363 --> 00:17:15,645
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, by definition, something that

170
00:17:16,647 --> 00:17:20,888
[SPEAKER_01]: You establish it for a moment, and then it will endure for who knows how long.

171
00:17:21,289 --> 00:17:33,032
