00:32:22,121 --> 00:32:26,584
[SPEAKER_01]: You have these perceptions of the forest.

347
00:32:27,705 --> 00:32:29,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it would even say,

348
00:32:30,953 --> 00:32:35,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, you just contemplate the root of a tree.

349
00:32:35,995 --> 00:32:39,516
[SPEAKER_01]: You expand your mind using the perception of the root of a tree.

350
00:32:40,516 --> 00:32:41,697
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's also wider.

351
00:32:42,097 --> 00:32:46,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, from the root of a tree, you expand it to the forest.

352
00:32:46,819 --> 00:32:48,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, from the forest, you expand it to the village.

353
00:32:49,280 --> 00:32:51,340
[SPEAKER_01]: From the village, you expand it to the country.

354
00:32:52,101 --> 00:32:54,422
[SPEAKER_01]: And all of these are essentially the same thing.

355
00:32:54,542 --> 00:32:54,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just...

356
00:32:55,880 --> 00:33:01,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Contextualizing whatever's happening in your mind with that broader thing that's already there.

357
00:33:01,981 --> 00:33:04,122
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't depend on you maintaining it.

358
00:33:04,582 --> 00:33:04,722
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

359
00:33:04,802 --> 00:33:11,504
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's actually why it's peaceful, because that perspective then, it's not like I'm doing this every moment.

360
00:33:11,684 --> 00:33:11,744
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

361
00:33:11,764 --> 00:33:12,864
[SPEAKER_01]: You are recollecting.

362
00:33:13,004 --> 00:33:14,564
[SPEAKER_03]: You're ceasing to ignore it.

363
00:33:14,604 --> 00:33:25,667
[SPEAKER_03]: That there's always a possibility of establishing a step back, quote-unquote, which is this context, which is what puts the perspective upon what's happening right now.

364
00:33:25,947 --> 00:33:31,251
[SPEAKER_01]: It shuts light on it, without you needing to put it there.

365
00:33:31,271 --> 00:33:33,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Something that was already there, you just recognize it.

366
00:33:33,932 --> 00:33:34,793
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the principle.

367
00:33:35,053 --> 00:33:41,558
[SPEAKER_01]: If it feels like I'm just doing this and maintaining this, that's not the right way.

368
00:33:41,578 --> 00:33:49,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if the idea about it is right, but it means you're not seeing it really as something that's in your experience, you're sort of fabricating it.

369
00:33:49,708 --> 00:33:56,034
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so you're not fabricating it, but the effort has to be in recollecting that it is already there.

370
00:33:56,735 --> 00:34:04,862
[SPEAKER_03]: So, perhaps, we can discuss now some, let's say, ways that will help

371
00:34:05,543 --> 00:34:06,704
[SPEAKER_03]: To not fall away from this.

372
00:34:07,424 --> 00:34:11,205
[SPEAKER_03]: Which, practically speaking, will mean, like, how is one going to recollect this?

373
00:34:11,606 --> 00:34:11,846
[SPEAKER_03]: Right?

374
00:34:12,686 --> 00:34:19,929
[SPEAKER_03]: So, we have cleared up to some degree this concurrency and the perspective, but one will be falling away from it.

375
00:34:20,469 --> 00:34:24,351
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how to practically not fall away from that?

376
00:34:25,131 --> 00:34:25,892
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the first thing.

377
00:34:26,232 --> 00:34:26,452
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

378
00:34:35,105 --> 00:34:35,846
[SPEAKER_02]: Keep your virtue.

379
00:34:38,007 --> 00:34:41,709
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't act out in the, like, super coarse obvious ways.

380
00:34:43,170 --> 00:34:46,412
[SPEAKER_02]: Also, don't act out in the less obvious ways either.

381
00:34:52,655 --> 00:34:52,976
[SPEAKER_03]: And then?

382
00:34:54,216 --> 00:34:55,197
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you come off to that?

383
00:34:58,579 --> 00:35:00,520
[SPEAKER_02]: Also, don't act out of the question.

384
00:35:00,540 --> 00:35:01,541
[SPEAKER_02]: All right, what now?

385
00:35:05,115 --> 00:35:13,457
[SPEAKER_04]: I would say that one will, like, fall out from this middle way, like, many times.

386
00:35:13,737 --> 00:35:14,557
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, many, many times.

387
00:35:15,337 --> 00:35:19,759
[SPEAKER_04]: And, at some point, he might feel like, oh, I'm not going to fall away anymore.

388
00:35:20,339 --> 00:35:21,219
[SPEAKER_04]: And then I can relax.

389
00:35:21,859 --> 00:35:23,539
[SPEAKER_04]: But that is already falling away.

390
00:35:23,840 --> 00:35:25,540
[SPEAKER_04]: So, instead of trying to, like,

391
00:35:28,800 --> 00:35:30,441
[SPEAKER_04]: I think it's like the Buddha used an image.

392
00:35:30,521 --> 00:35:34,623
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like the guy who guards the cow.

393
00:35:34,843 --> 00:35:36,784
[SPEAKER_04]: He's kind of feeding them to not go into the crops.

394
00:35:37,284 --> 00:35:41,386
[SPEAKER_04]: And when they don't go into the crops, they don't just lie down and fall asleep.

395
00:35:41,726 --> 00:35:45,528
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like he lies down, he can relax, but he keeps an eye on them.

396
00:35:47,109 --> 00:35:55,593
[SPEAKER_04]: He can, to some degree, relax, but he still has to have in mind that as long as he's not free from the liability from these things to happen,

397
00:35:56,553 --> 00:35:58,234
[SPEAKER_04]: That can still, to some degree, happen.

398
00:35:58,254 --> 00:36:04,998
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I'm still sitting there, watching my cow, and... but it's still much more peaceful than before.

399
00:36:06,019 --> 00:36:08,220
[SPEAKER_04]: So, being vigilant, I would say, is a good kind of...

400
00:36:08,800 --> 00:36:23,048
[SPEAKER_04]: When one, like, kind of, manages to understand, like, this kind of recollection of this kind of direction between content and context, is to, kind of, every time he falls, to come back, but when he's not falling back,

401
00:36:25,930 --> 00:36:31,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you understood it rightly, you will realize that it's... again, these things are already in your experience.

402
00:36:31,572 --> 00:36:40,475
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it feels like being vigilant or maintaining it is like painful or like it's a burden, then you haven't understood it properly, because it's like...

403
00:36:41,545 --> 00:36:47,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, your act of forgetting about these things comes second to these things already being there.

404
00:36:48,088 --> 00:36:56,412
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, it's... even your forgetting is kind of like irrelevant, because it's fundamentally still there.

405
00:36:57,513 --> 00:37:00,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Irrelevant in the sense that... Yeah, yeah, of course.

406
00:37:00,955 --> 00:37:05,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, it doesn't mean like you're justified in forgetting, but what it means is like...

407
00:37:06,487 --> 00:37:15,669
[SPEAKER_01]: If you emphasize, again, the aspect of, no, no, it's only there when I am thinking about it, then, even if you're thinking about it, it won't be on the right level.

408
00:37:17,549 --> 00:37:25,271
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to basically see, like, yes, if I... even if I were to completely forget it, it would still be there.

409
00:37:25,911 --> 00:37:27,612
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean that you allow yourself to forget.

410
00:37:27,632 --> 00:37:27,892
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

411
00:37:28,312 --> 00:37:30,092
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't mean you're already enlightened.

412
00:37:30,172 --> 00:37:30,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

413
00:37:33,773 --> 00:37:35,494
[SPEAKER_03]: And now there's this other sutra which occurs to me.

414
00:37:35,734 --> 00:37:47,300
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, there's this simile where somebody walks around with a bowl of water on his head, which is like filled to the brim, and then there's a big market square, and then there's a lot of people there,

415
00:37:47,925 --> 00:38:03,003
[SPEAKER_03]: And then there are some enticing women performing some show, and then there's somebody walking behind you with a raised sword, and if there's only one drop of water that spills from that bowl, then that assassin will just chop your head off on the spot.

416
00:38:04,004 --> 00:38:06,267
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, this bowl being full of water,

417
00:38:07,088 --> 00:38:16,516
[SPEAKER_03]: It can be interpreted as like, you know, you want to keep your mindfulness and not lose it even for one drop, but that assassin is again behind you, right?

418
00:38:16,796 --> 00:38:21,980
[SPEAKER_03]: So, mindfulness of death, in that sense, is also a context that you can establish.

419
00:38:23,181 --> 00:38:31,206
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's also something that you find yourself within, which, when similarly is widely understood, can... is basically on that same level.

420
00:38:31,506 --> 00:38:33,627
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not that you're creating your own death.

421
00:38:34,067 --> 00:38:36,348
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't die, because you're also already liable to it.

422
00:38:36,428 --> 00:38:48,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and also, as I said, those who are not mindful are as if dead, in the sense that anything that comes then, it's like... it's... it's like a floodgate that's open.

423
00:38:49,075 --> 00:38:50,256
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, anything that comes in,

424
00:38:51,040 --> 00:38:55,582
[SPEAKER_01]: is going to overwhelm you, including, most importantly, death.

425
00:38:58,063 --> 00:39:11,808
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, it is something that, because it doesn't depend on the content of your experience, it is actually realistic to live with that attitude of like, I must not forget this, even for a moment.

426
00:39:12,945 --> 00:39:30,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas, if it were about focusing, you kind of can't do that, because you won't be able to have this object... like, first of all, sit with your eyes closed every moment, which is most of the time required in order to focus on something.

427
00:39:31,440 --> 00:39:34,343
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's this context that stands below everything,

428
00:39:35,003 --> 00:39:38,544
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no justification for losing it, because you don't need to...

429
00:39:38,564 --> 00:39:41,645
[SPEAKER_03]: It's much more stable, actually, in its nature, compared to the other direction.

430
00:39:41,665 --> 00:39:41,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

431
00:39:42,025 --> 00:39:47,687
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't need to... you don't need to forget about what you're feeling in order to do physical work.

432
00:39:47,907 --> 00:39:50,548
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to forget about what you're feeling in order to talk.

433
00:39:50,708 --> 00:39:53,169
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to forget about what you're feeling in order to do anything.

434
00:39:53,829 --> 00:40:01,072
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there isn't a justification for ever losing that context, and you can actually then live in that way of perfect vigilance.

435
00:40:01,952 --> 00:40:02,412
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

436
00:40:03,884 --> 00:40:08,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's... the other thing I wanted to clear up regarding that simile is...

437
00:40:10,858 --> 00:40:33,835
[SPEAKER_01]: In the context of the practice, let's say, when you actually sit down and dedicate yourself to these reflections, as it would say in seclusion, of course, in an empty hut, in a forest or something, you then have to not take the simile literally, in the sense of, okay, let me now have this

438
00:40:34,877 --> 00:40:42,405
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that particular thought of these satipaṭṭhānas present every second, because otherwise my head will be cut off.

439
00:40:43,206 --> 00:40:44,687
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that perspective.

440
00:40:45,508 --> 00:40:50,593
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you have to think about it for a moment, because it's not like it's going to be magically there, but on its own.

441
00:40:52,317 --> 00:40:55,359
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to think about it, bring it to your mind, attend to it, let's say.

442
00:40:56,200 --> 00:41:08,391
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if you saw it properly, because it is actually already there in your experience, sort of the memory becomes like embedded into your experience then and then.

443
00:41:08,531 --> 00:41:14,356
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, your goal is now, okay, how do I not lose the perspective

444
00:41:15,741 --> 00:41:20,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of, how do I just keep this thought about the perspective every second?

445
00:41:20,385 --> 00:41:23,587
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, because then you're controlling again, which is maintenance.

446
00:41:23,647 --> 00:41:33,474
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's going to lose... like, if you're doing it that way, that kind of already implies that you didn't see the perspective to begin with, because it would become obvious that that's not where it is.

447
00:41:33,794 --> 00:41:36,416
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in me thinking about the feeling.

448
00:41:37,457 --> 00:41:41,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the feeling is not in me, every second, attending to the feeling.

449
00:41:41,521 --> 00:41:45,825
[SPEAKER_01]: The feeling is the foundation for my experience, and I just need to recognize it.

450
00:41:46,225 --> 00:41:55,133
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, in that sense, mindfulness, once established, has to become independent of your doing, until it goes away, and then you bring it back.

451
00:41:55,823 --> 00:41:58,104
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it goes away again, and then you bring it back, and so on.

452
00:41:58,464 --> 00:41:59,745
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, how does it go away?

453
00:42:00,545 --> 00:42:06,448
[SPEAKER_03]: Because understanding how it goes away might practically prevent it from going away.

454
00:42:06,608 --> 00:42:09,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what... yeah, you could say, what's the measure for it going away?

455
00:42:09,710 --> 00:42:09,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

456
00:42:09,950 --> 00:42:16,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it doesn't depend on on you thinking about it, then how do we define when the mindfulness went away?

457
00:42:17,093 --> 00:42:18,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's exactly the question.

458
00:42:18,634 --> 00:42:18,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

459
00:42:35,145 --> 00:42:48,777
[SPEAKER_02]: Because sometimes it can go away for, like, a few weeks, even, just, like, losing perspective and just kind of like... Well, relatively speaking, like, the more subtle aspects you might have lost.

460
00:42:49,995 --> 00:42:50,916
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the good news.

461
00:42:51,316 --> 00:42:55,379
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're established in virtue, some degree of perspective will never leave you.

462
00:42:56,099 --> 00:42:57,560
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

463
00:42:57,620 --> 00:43:13,472
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, the more subtle things that you're still sort of trying to understand, you might lose those for a while, but the... virtue is already a form of mindfulness in that sense, because not doing those things, in and of itself, involves greater perspective.

464
00:43:17,972 --> 00:43:20,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, we could take the example of virtue.

465
00:43:21,275 --> 00:43:26,979
[SPEAKER_01]: When do you lose the mindfulness of virtue, let's say?

466
00:43:27,640 --> 00:43:35,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it when you forget that you stop repeating the mantra of the precepts in your mind?

467
00:43:36,507 --> 00:43:36,987
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's not.

468
00:43:41,823 --> 00:43:43,223
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

469
00:43:43,283 --> 00:43:56,587
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you find yourself acting out, mentally, intentionally, out of greed, aversion or delusion, like, let's say, five hindrances, that's when you know.

470
00:43:57,307 --> 00:43:58,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Indirectly, pretty much.

471
00:43:58,748 --> 00:44:02,689
[SPEAKER_01]: You know indirectly, sort of by inference, oh, the context went away.

472
00:44:02,949 --> 00:44:04,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it's time to bring it back.

473
00:44:04,730 --> 00:44:06,590
[SPEAKER_01]: But, for a while, you will have that there.

474
00:44:06,970 --> 00:44:08,251
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yes, you will have, like, the

475
00:44:09,446 --> 00:44:11,627
[SPEAKER_01]: The attempts of the hindrances, let's say.

476
00:44:12,128 --> 00:44:16,830
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as that light of the context is there, they won't be able to find footing.

477
00:44:16,970 --> 00:44:20,873
[SPEAKER_01]: But, at some point, it will happen that you lost it, and they will come back.

478
00:44:20,993 --> 00:44:23,434
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you know, oh, I had to switch on the light again.

479
00:44:23,614 --> 00:44:23,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

480
00:44:24,495 --> 00:44:24,815
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

481
00:44:25,315 --> 00:44:34,664
[SPEAKER_04]: So, we lose them by... we lose this perspective by... not directly, but like by engaging in certain things that require us to forget about it.

482
00:44:34,684 --> 00:44:34,924
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

483
00:44:35,245 --> 00:44:44,994
[SPEAKER_01]: You will... you will find yourself in like a... some establishment of perspective, and then something will come that feels like it's justified.

484
00:44:45,832 --> 00:44:49,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Might even be like an idea about, oh, let me clarify this idea of dhamma now.

485
00:44:49,873 --> 00:44:53,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I know that this is the practice right here, this perspective.

486
00:44:54,075 --> 00:45:06,679
[SPEAKER_01]: I know that this is actually the dhamma, but my mind convinces me like, no, no, it's these, you know, abstract scholarly connections that I need to make between also how does

487
00:45:07,539 --> 00:45:14,484
[SPEAKER_01]: The third satipaṭṭhāna relates to the, you know, the right livelihood or something.

488
00:45:14,844 --> 00:45:18,166
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but why is that problematic?

489
00:45:18,246 --> 00:45:22,289
[SPEAKER_03]: Because in order to make those connections or comparisons, you are...

490
00:45:22,329 --> 00:45:23,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Descending to the content again.

491
00:45:24,110 --> 00:45:26,413
[SPEAKER_03]: That's exactly what I would say in those words.

492
00:45:27,814 --> 00:45:32,358
[SPEAKER_03]: There cannot be a comparison between backgrounds and foregrounds, from that point of view.

493
00:45:32,959 --> 00:45:33,960
[SPEAKER_03]: They depend on one another.

494
00:45:33,980 --> 00:45:39,104
[SPEAKER_03]: But the comparison that we are talking about, that makes you fall away from the establishment, will always be

495
00:45:39,905 --> 00:45:41,907
[SPEAKER_03]: That way, and then start making comparisons there.

496
00:45:42,708 --> 00:45:49,554
[SPEAKER_03]: So that... so you... so you want to go from that horizontal view over there, to re-establish a perspective which is that way, basically.

497
00:45:50,815 --> 00:46:05,128
[SPEAKER_03]: So, to get into the habit of these... of... well, to get into the habit of recognizing this tendency to compare, is useful, because when you recognize that, it can help to re-establish that perspective.

498
00:46:05,228 --> 00:46:16,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, when you're established in it, you kind of have a sense, if you're self-honest, you have a sense of what will take you away and what will not take you away.

499
00:46:17,470 --> 00:46:29,393
[SPEAKER_01]: And the last thing I wanted to mention is, that's why the virtue is necessary beforehand, because even though you might have, as the suttas would say, like a finger snap of the right perspective,

500
00:46:30,389 --> 00:46:48,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Realistically speaking, if you're not established in virtue, all these other things that will sort of distract you from it, will be so convincing for you, like, you're just not used to not being trapped by those baits, that, realistically speaking, that perspective is going to last very little.

501
00:46:48,055 --> 00:46:51,096
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, even if you're repeating the same ideas and the same concepts,

502
00:46:52,842 --> 00:47:04,077
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see the difference, because again, you will be approaching it through the angle of ideas, or the same concept of establishing the context, but it's not in the right level now, because...

503
00:47:04,601 --> 00:47:11,146
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so the idea of the context doesn't necessarily mean that you are now attending, with the right context, to the idea of the context.

504
00:47:11,186 --> 00:47:11,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

505
00:47:11,566 --> 00:47:24,915
[SPEAKER_01]: So, virtue and sensory training and all these things, they just get you used to, even if you're not explicitly thinking about it that way, they get, they make you get used to not operating as much on the level of content anymore.

506
00:47:25,116 --> 00:47:25,316
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

507
00:47:25,336 --> 00:47:29,859
[SPEAKER_01]: That basically, like, that's the most direct way that you can guarantee

508
00:47:30,552 --> 00:47:38,412
[SPEAKER_01]: That when you try to practice these recollections, that you won't go in the wrong direction of clarifying ideas.

